      "beforeContent": "\"\"\"Guard-side client of the SAIPEN host-authority broker.\n\nThe Claude Code hook and the canonical engine never mint admission, and this\nmodule is where they stop pretending they can. It asks the broker, and the\nbroker answers after proving from the operating system that the request comes\nfrom the host's own hook invocation. Nothing here decides an admission state:\nthe verdict, the capability and the ledger record all come back from the\nbroker, and a request this module cannot deliver is reported as a typed\nfailure rather than invented.\n\nRecovery is bounded and explicit. When no broker answers, the client attempts\nexactly one automatic start and retries the handshake once. Every step is\nrecorded in the returned `recovery` block so the diagnostic the operator sees\ncan name the component that failed and the recovery that was attempted,\ninstead of repeating that an authority \"is required\".\n\nThe server is authenticated too. An endpoint name is only a name: before a\nrequest is sent on the operator's real endpoint, the process serving it must be\nthe SAIPEN service entry of a regi
---
FILE: C:\Users\vac34\.zcode\cli\artifacts\sess_389151f2-976c-46f2-ae9a-4857d39121d8\call_00_lu0h9huyn15rf869p9z0s20h-tool-result-77649d59-614c-4eca-b2cb-921f88e4af0b.json
{
  "createdAt": "2026-10-28T15:52:57.528Z",
  "files": [
    {
      "beforeContent": "\"\"\"The SAIPEN host-authority broker: the separated local admission authority.\n\nDefect class this closes: T-1563 established that a same-user Python hook cannot\nprove host provenance, because the hook and any model-executed Python run as the\nsame OS principal, so every locally minted token is self-attestation. That\nconclusion is right, and on its own it left the operator with a GUI that could\nnot be used: Claude Code's prompt boundary refused every prompt and the only\nhonest diagnostic was \"a separated host authority is required\".\n\nThis module IS that separated authority -- a local service that\n\n* runs as its own process, outside the hook's process, and outlives a Claude GUI\n  restart;\n* keeps its signing key in memory only, so no writable file is authority and no\n  capability can be forged from readable bytes;\n* identifies the client by OS-derived process identity rather than by anything\n  the client sends, and accepts exactly one shape: the host application's own\n  hook invocation (`host_authority_identity`). \"Somewhere under the host\" is\n  not that shape -- a command t
---
